<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anne Frank</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anne Frank was a German-born Jewish girl whose family fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and, from 1942, hid from the Nazi occupiers in a secret annex behind her father's business. During two years in hiding she kept a diary. The family was betrayed and arrested in August 1944, and she died in Bergen-Belsen in early 1945. Her diary, published in 1947, became one of the most widely read books of the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anne-frank.jpg" alt="Anne Frank" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anne_Frank_passport_photo,_May_1942_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Annelies Marie Frank was born on 12 June 1929 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, the younger daughter of Edith Frank, née Holländer, and Otto Heinrich Frank. Her sister was Margot. The family were Reform Jews who lived among a mixed community of Jewish and non-Jewish citizens, and her parents, who encouraged reading, kept an extensive library. In 1934, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> had been appointed to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, among about 300,000 Jews who left Germany between 1933 and 1939. Anne joined the 6th Montessori School there. German occupation trapped the family in May 1940, and in 1941 she lost her German citizenship and became stateless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>For her thirteenth birthday on 12 June 1942 Anne received an autograph book with a small lock, which she decided to use as a diary and named Kitty. On 6 July 1942, as persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> increased, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> moved into a hiding place, the Achterhuis or Secret Annex, a three-storey space entered from a landing above the Opekta offices on the Prinsengracht, where Otto Frank's trusted employees acted as their helpers. A bookcase later covered the door. In the diary she wrote about the restrictions on Dutch Jews and about the strongly differing personalities of the people sharing the annex, and about her relationships with her family. She grew closer to Margot as she matured, and was closest emotionally to her father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 4 August 1944 German police led by SS-Oberscharführer Karl Silberbauer raided the annex. How it was found is uncertain: a biography of 2015 suggested a betrayal, and research published by the Anne Frank House in 2016 pointed to an investigation of ration-card fraud as a possible cause, without ruling out betrayal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were taken to Westerbork and, on the last transport from there, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> on 3 September 1944. On 1 November 1944 Anne and Margot were moved to Bergen-Belsen, where they died, probably of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>, in February or March 1945.</p><p>Otto Frank was the only member of the family to survive. Miep Gies and Bep Voskuijl had saved Anne's notebooks, and in July 1945 Gies gave them to him. He published the diary in 1947; it appeared in English in 1952 as The Diary of a Young Girl. It has been translated into more than 70 languages and is the basis of several plays and films. Later editions restored passages cut from the first, including pages removed by Otto Frank himself. Opened on 3 May 1960, the Anne Frank House preserves the Prinsengracht building.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anne Frank” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…left lasting marks: about 75 per cent of the Jews of the Netherlands were murdered in the Holocaust, one of the highest proportions in Western Europe, and the diary of <b>Anne Frank</b>, written in hiding in Amsterdam, became one of the most widely read books in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Composers have quoted it to evoke the regime, among them Karlheinz Stockhausen in Hymnen (1967) and Lukas Foss in his Elegy for <b>Anne Frank</b> (1989).</blockquote><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Maingau Clinic of the Red Cross, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Bergen-Belsen concentration camp, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.12,8.69&amp;year=1939" aria-label="Maingau Clinic of the Red Cross on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4749 421 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anne Frank"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5199 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5199 691h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.12,8.69&amp;year=1939">What was here in 1939? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
